course: everyday
lesson: 484
title: Enemies who became friends, and ones who never did
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Dean has had it in for Sam since Year 2. In Year 5 the teacher pairs them for the science fair: «Miss, no way.» Dean turns out to be brilliant at building and Sam at explaining, and their bridge holds four bricks and wins. They start sitting together at lunch. Dean still calls him «Sam-wise», but now it sounds friendly. Connor and Tyler, rival strikers for two schools, both get picked for the county under-11s: «Pass it, then!» «You pass it!» By the third match they're scoring off each other's passes. Working together can turn enemies into mates.
explain: Mia's worst enemy, Harper, moves in next door, and their mums decide the girls will walk to school together. «Literally kill me now,» says Mia, which means «this is terrible». After a month of walks they find they both hate the same teacher's jokes and love the same band. Friends by Halloween. Maddie and Ellie hated each other all through Year 3; in Year 4 Ellie's dad dies suddenly, and Maddie, not knowing what to do, makes a card with a drawing of Ellie's dog. Ellie keeps it in her bag for a year. Not best friends, but the war is over for good.
explain: Some enemies stay enemies. Kieran and Jake have been tried by everyone, teachers and mums: «He's just a wasteman,» says Kieran (London slang for a useless person). They agree, without saying so, to stay out of each other's way: no fights, no friendship, just a nod. After Grace spread Lily's secret, Lily tried to make up once, and Grace laughed. Lily finds kind friends, and seeing Grace in the corridor stops hurting by Christmas. Chloe and Bella are still frenemies in Year 6 (a frenemy is nice alone, nasty in a group); Chloe stays polite but stops sharing secrets.
explain: Some peace comes years later. Big Gaz, who kicked Sam's ball in the river, becomes a youth football coach and tells Sam's dad, «Tell your Sam I'm sorry for being a little toerag back then» (a nasty little rascal). Sam laughs: it's fine now. Sam's dad's old school bully found him on Facebook twenty years on: «I was horrible to you. I'm sorry. I was having a hard time at home.» Dad wrote back, «Thanks, mate. All good.» And Mill Lane and Station Road, who fought for years, hold a joint street party with bunting, a bouncy castle and a tug of war that ends in a draw and a heap of kids.
fact: Working together on a shared task can turn enemies into friends.
fact: Rivals who respect each other's skill can become good teammates.
fact: Spending time close together sometimes turns enemies into friends; it is worth giving it time before deciding.
fact: Kindness at someone's saddest time, like a card when a parent dies, can end a long feud.
fact: Not every enemy becomes a friend; peaceful distance, with no fights and a polite nod, is a fine ending too.
fact: Not every broken friendship can be fixed; it is all right to let it go and look after the good ones.
fact: Some people keep being unkind; you can stay polite but keep your distance and your secrets.
fact: People can change as they grow up, and an old sorry still counts.
fact: Some people who bullied others were having a hard time themselves; that explains it but doesn't make it OK.
fact: It is never too late to say sorry, or to accept one.
fact: A shared event, like a street party, can help two groups make peace.
fact: «Wasteman» means a useless person, «toerag» means a nasty little rascal, and a «frenemy» is a friend who is also partly an enemy.
exercise: How did Dean and Sam become friends? | Working together on the science fair | A teacher made them shake hands in front of the class | Dean lost a fight | Their mums became friends
exercise: What was each of them good at for the bridge? | Dean at building, Sam at explaining | Sam at building, Dean at explaining | Both at painting | Neither, the teacher did it
exercise: How did Maddie end the war with Ellie? | A card with a drawing of Ellie's dog when her dad died | A big present on her birthday | Beating her in a race on sports day in front of everyone | Telling a teacher about her
exercise: Kieran and Jake never got on. How did it end? | Staying out of each other's way, just a nod | A huge fight at break in front of the whole school | Becoming best friends | One of them moved schools
exercise: What happened when Lily tried to make up with Grace? | Grace laughed, and they never really spoke again | They became best friends again by the end of the week | Grace said sorry at once | Lily spread a rumour back
exercise: How did Lily feel by Christmas? | Seeing Grace stopped hurting | Still crying about it every day | She wanted revenge on Grace and Bella | She missed Grace terribly every single day
exercise: Connor and Tyler were rival strikers. What happened at county level? | They became a good team scoring off each other | They refused to pass to each other all season | Tyler was dropped | They had a fight on the pitch
exercise: Mia's enemy Harper moved next door. What happened after a month of walks? | They became friends | They stopped walking together | They had a big fight | Harper moved away again
exercise: What is a frenemy? | Someone nice alone and nasty in a group | A very old friend you have known forever | A friend from another country | An enemy who never speaks
exercise: What did Chloe do about Bella, who never changed? | Stayed friendly but stopped sharing secrets | Became her best friend and told her everything | Started a fight with her | Moved schools
exercise: What did Big Gaz say years later? | Sorry for being a little toerag | That Sam still owed him a football from Year 4 | That he didn't remember Sam | That Sam was a crybaby
exercise: How did the two streets make peace? | A joint street party | A court case | A big football match with a fight after | Moving away
exercise: What did Sam's dad's old bully write twenty years later? | That he was sorry and had a hard time at home | That he wanted to meet up and fight again | That he was still angry | That Dad still owed him money from school
exercise: How did Sam's dad answer the late sorry? | «Thanks, mate. All good» | He never replied | He blocked him | He demanded a meeting
exercise: What does «wasteman» mean? | A useless person | A man who collects the bins | A rich man | A football fan
exercise: Which is a fine ending when an enemy never becomes a friend? | Peaceful distance with a polite nod | A fight to decide who wins | Revenge when nobody's looking | Spreading rumours about them so others take your side
test: «The teacher paired me with my worst enemy, literally kill me now». What might happen? | Working together could turn them into mates | They'll definitely fight all term | The project will fail for sure | The teacher wants to punish them
test: «Every enemy becomes a friend in the end, like in films». True? | No, some stay enemies, and peaceful distance is fine | Yes, always, if you wait long enough | Yes, if you keep giving them presents and sweets until they like you | No, enemies always get worse
test: «Ellie's dad died but she's my enemy so it's not my problem». What did Maddie's card show? | Kindness at a sad time can end a war | Enemies should be ignored when they are sad | Cards are for best friends only | Maddie wanted something from Ellie in return
test: «Bella's sound when it's just us, then she's horrible in the group». How should Chloe handle a frenemy? | Stay polite but don't share secrets | Tell her everything to win her over | Be horrible back in the group | Copy what Bella does
test: «Gaz said sorry after ten years, that doesn't count». Does it? | Yes, an old sorry still counts | No, sorries expire after a year | No, only sorries in person count | Yes, but only if he pays for the ball
test: «he only bullied me cos things were bad at home, so it was fine». Is that right? | It explains it, but it wasn't OK | Yes, a hard time makes it fine | No, he made up the excuse | Yes, so Dad should apologise
test: «Grace laughed at my sorry so I'll never have any friends». What happened to Lily? | She found new, kind friends | She stayed alone for years | Grace became her best friend | She moved to another town
test: What would probably happen if Connor and Tyler refused to pass all season? | The county team would lose more | They'd both be top scorers | Their rivalry would end | The coach would make them captains
test: «our street and Station Road hate each other, always will, innit». What does the street party show? | Peace between groups is possible | Parties make fights worse | Bouncy castles cause wars | The streets still hate each other
test: In a cartoon, two enemies are forced to share a house and become best friends in five minutes. What's more real? | It can happen, but it takes time | It always happens in five minutes | It never happens at all | Only grown-ups can do it
test: «Dean still calls me Sam-wise, so we can't be friends». Why is it different now? | It sounds friendly now, not mean | He's still bullying him | Nicknames mean you're enemies | Sam should report him
exam: Identify the principal means, as established in the lesson, by which two pupils of long-standing enmity became friends. | Collaboration upon a shared task | Compulsory reconciliation ordered by a teacher | A physical contest decided in favour of one | The intervention of their parents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an enmity that does not develop into friendship? | Peaceable distance, without conflict, constitutes an acceptable resolution | It must be concluded by a contest | It must be pursued by retaliation until one party concedes defeat | It indicates failure on the part of both parties
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning an apology tendered many years after the offence. | It retains its value and may be accepted | It is void by reason of the long delay | It obliges the recipient to resume the dispute | It is acceptable only if delivered in person
exam: Ascertain the most prudent conduct towards a peer who is agreeable in private but unkind in company. | To remain courteous whilst withholding confidences | To confide in her more fully in order to win her | To retaliate in company | To imitate her conduct
